# CLI reference

The workspace provides `rocci` for template modules and runtime applications, `rocdown` for Markdown documents and static sites, `rocci-okf` for knowledge bundle review, and `rocci-browser` for a persistent project picker. Direct `run` still opens a one-shot preview window.

Examples below use `cargo run` from a source checkout. Installed binaries use
the same arguments without the Cargo prefix.

## Rocci CLI

### Validate configuration

```sh
cargo run -p rocci-cli -- validate [rocci.toml]
```

Parse and validate the application configuration. Unknown fields and unsafe
runtime settings are errors.

### Build .rocci template to Roc

```sh
cargo run -p rocci-cli -- build INPUT [-o OUTPUT]
```

Compile a single `.rocci` template to Roc. With `-o`, write to the given path;
otherwise print to stdout.

### Inspect template source and AST

```sh
cargo run -p rocci-cli -- inspect INPUT [--ast] [--segments]
```

Inspect compiled information: component signatures, fixtures, styles, handlers,
and source map segments. `--ast` prints the parsed AST as an S-expression.

### Run a single component

```sh
cargo run -p rocci-cli -- view INPUT [--component NAME] [--arg name=val] [--port PORT] [--no-window]
```

Run a component with mock arguments and an optional state type. The component
is wrapped in a minimal application and launched in a native window.

### Browse components

```sh
cargo run -p rocci-cli -- browse [DIRECTORY] [--port PORT] [--no-window]
```

Discover and interactively browse all `.rocci` components and fixtures in a directory.

### Run an application

```sh
cargo run -p rocci-cli -- run [ENTRY] [--port PORT] [--no-window] [--args...]
```

Run the entry template or directory. The application server starts and opens a
preview window by default. The preview overlay supports current-document find
(`Cmd/Ctrl-F`, `E` for selection, `G` / `Shift-G` to move, wrapping at the
ends) and go-to-file (`Cmd/Ctrl-K`). Static site and knowledge review servers
expose a Dev control that docks an inspector with Performance, Source, and
Console tabs (original source, AST, generated Roc, generated HTML, plus
runtime session logs). Source scrolls long Roc/HTML and highlights Rocci,
Rocdown, Markdown, Roc, and HTML with `tok-*` classes. Dock is right or
bottom only. `rocci run` serves that
panel on a sibling loopback port so it is not injected into the authored app's
routes. `--no-window` still keeps the sibling inspector and captures HTML
after listen; it prints timings on stderr instead of opening a window.

| Option | Effect |
| --- | --- |
| `--no-window` | Serve without opening the preview window |
| `--port PORT` | Override the listening port (defaults to a free port, or 8000 with `--no-window`) |
| `--args...` | Extra arguments forwarded to the Roc binary |

### Bundle a macOS app

```sh
cargo run -p rocci-cli -- bundle [--config rocci.toml] [--target x64musl|arm64musl]
```

Build a local ad-hoc-signed macOS app bundle. `--target` is the Roc native
ISA/OS for Linux process binaries (`x64musl` is the Linux container target).
macOS `.app` bundles stay host-native and reject `--target`. `--host` on
`rocdown build` is apply-only and is not this flag.

### Datastar asset management

```sh
cargo run -p rocci-cli -- datastar pin [VERSION] [--app PATH]
cargo run -p rocci-cli -- datastar update [--app PATH]
```

Pin or update the Datastar JavaScript runtime asset in an application directory.

---

## Rocdown CLI

### Run an interactive document or documentation site

```sh
# Run a single interactive document
cargo run -p rocci-rocdown-cli -- run report.rocdown

# Preview a page that lives inside a documentation site
cargo run -p rocci-rocdown-cli -- run docs/guides/docs-components.rocdown

# Run a documentation site directory with live reload
cargo run -p rocci-rocdown-cli -- run [ROOT] [--port PORT] [--no-window] [--output DIRECTORY]
```

Watch `.rocdown` sources, `rocdown.toml`, and assets, rebuild, and serve the site
with live reload. Hybrid sites with colocated `@on` handlers also start the
generated island service and proxy `/actions/` on this same origin so Datastar
posts work locally. Sibling `[http].service` apps still run with
`rocdown serve-islands` or `rocci run`. The default root is the current
directory. A `.rocdown` file under an ancestor `rocdown.toml` uses that site
root and opens the page route, so includes and `/docs/...` links resolve the
same way as `rocdown run docs`. A file with no ancestor config stays a
standalone document. Preview output
goes to a temp directory unless `--output` is set. The preview window supports
current-document find and `Cmd/Ctrl-K` jump-to-page using `/pages.json`. The
Dev control opens a panel at `/__rocci/dev` with Performance, Source, and
Console tabs for the current route (`?tab=`, `?route=`, and `?view=`). Long
Source Roc and HTML scroll inside the pane. Original Rocci/Rocdown/Markdown,
generated Roc, and generated HTML use `tok-*` highlighting; AST stays plain.
Console
shows runtime session messages, not an app log API. `rocdown run` and
`rocdown build` refuse OKF knowledge records and bundles and point to
`rocci-okf run`.

| Option | Effect |
| --- | --- |
| `--no-window` | Serve without opening the preview window |
| `--port PORT` | Override the listening port |
| `--output DIRECTORY` | Write preview files here instead of a temp directory |

### Build documentation

```sh
cargo run -p rocci-rocdown-cli -- build [ROOT] [--output DIRECTORY] [--cdn-only] [--host auto|native|wasm] [--target x64musl|arm64musl]
```

The default root is the current directory. `ROOT` may be a documentation
directory or a `.rocdown` file. `rocdown.toml` is optional. `--output` overrides
`build.output`. OKF knowledge records are refused here as well. `--cdn-only`
errors (`RD2302`) when any published page is `live`, so a CDN-only publish cannot
ship Datastar actions without the island service. Default hybrid build accepts
`live` pages and prints page kinds, whether Datastar was emitted, and service
routes.

`--host` selects how **apply** runs on the build machine (`native` uses
`basic-cli` without a Roc `--target`; `wasm` uses the embedded wasm32 renderer).
`--host wasm` does not produce a hosted Wasm server. Some Roc platforms cannot
compile to wasm, so native apply remains required. `--target` is a separate
native ISA/OS for **island and app** `main.roc` process binaries
(`x64musl` / `arm64musl` for Linux containers). Do not pass musl `--target`
into `--host native` on a Mac.

### Package a site

```sh
cargo run -p rocci-rocdown-cli -- package [ROOT] [--output DIRECTORY] [--archive site.tgz] [--target x64musl|arm64musl]
```

Implies `--cdn-only` for static catalogs. `live` pages fail with `RD2302`
when `--cdn-only` is set. Otherwise a hybrid package compiles the island
process binary (sibling `islands` file) and records live routes plus the
binary fingerprint in `publish.json`. `--target x64musl` / `arm64musl` is
the Linux container process target. Failed package leaves the previous
`site.tgz`. `--no-archive` skips the tarball.

### Serve a built tree

```sh
cargo run -p rocci-rocdown-cli -- serve [DIST] [--port PORT] [--no-window]
```

Serve an existing `dist/` on loopback without Roc, watch, or rebuild. `DIST`
must contain `index.html`. Preview window follows the same `--no-window` rules
as `rocdown run`. This checks that a package is self-contained; it is not
`rocdown run` (which rebuilds from sources) and not `serve-islands` (island
process only).

Local `:img` and Markdown image paths resolve against the source file. Missing
local files fail the catalog (`RD2103`). Files under `build.assets` are
content-hashed whether the authored URL is `/assets/foo.png` or `assets/foo.png`.

### Check documentation

```sh
cargo run -p rocci-rocdown-cli -- check [ROOT] [--format terminal|json]
```

Validate the catalog, including article-block content models, includes, page links,
and local assets, without compiling Roc or writing output.

### Test documented examples

```sh
cargo run -p rocci-rocdown-cli -- test [ROOT] [--update] [--format terminal|json]
```

Run `:example` commands that declare `test`. `--update` rewrites golden
files named by `expect`.

### Inspect Rocdown AST and planned artifacts

```sh
cargo run -p rocci-rocdown-cli -- inspect ast FILE.rocdown
cargo run -p rocci-rocdown-cli -- inspect artifacts [ROOT]
```

`inspect artifacts` reports page kinds, Datastar emission, island service
routes, and the planned output tree.

### Serve island actions

```sh
cargo run -p rocci-rocdown-cli -- serve-islands [ROOT] [--port PORT] [--no-window]
```

Start the island HTTP service for `live` pages by itself. Colocated `@on`
handlers compile into one dispatcher. A sibling `[http].service` `.rocci` app
is run with `rocci run` instead. The process serves `/health` and mutation
routes; it does not serve Markdown or the CDN HTML tree. Use this beside a
published `rocdown build` output. Local preview that needs both artifacts on
one origin is `rocdown run DIR`.

See [Publish a hybrid Rocdown site](/docs/guides/hybrid-sites/) for the
two-artifact deploy.

